From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 22:22:52 +0200
Subject: phy: intel: add GRX500 USB PHY driver

The xRX500 pairs each of its two Synopsys DWC3 controllers with a PHY
that has a gate clock, a reset line, an optional VBUS supply and one
field in the chip-top syscon. Nothing upstream covers it:
phy-intel-lgm-combo.c is an x86 SerDes with no USB mode at all,
phy-intel-keembay-usb.c is unrelated arm64 silicon, and
phy-lantiq-rcu-usb2.c stops at xrx300 and is unreachable from
INTEL_MIPS anyway, being gated on SOC_TYPE_XWAY.

That last one is still the right template, because the interesting
part of this hardware is the same on both SoC generations. The DWC3
core is little endian and this CPU is not, so the byte order of the
core's AXI master ports is selected outside the core, in a chip-top
register: bit 10 for the host-mode port, bit 9 for the device-mode
port. phy-lantiq-rcu-usb2.c calls those same two bit positions
host_endianness and slave_endianness, sets them from its phy .init,
and describes it as "Select DMA endianness (Host-endian: big-endian)".
This driver does the same thing at the same place, which is why the
port needs no dwc3 glue driver: with the endianness write owned by the
PHY, nothing SoC-specific is left for a glue layer to do, and the dwc3
core binds snps,dwc3 straight from DT.

The vendor arranges it differently. AVM 4.9 splits the work between
drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-grx500.c, a 161-line glue whose entire
SoC-specific content is that one register write followed by
of_platform_populate(), and drivers/phy/phy-grx500-usb.c, which owns
the clock, reset, regulator and polarity overrides. Merging the two
loses nothing: the glue's only other behaviour is an
of_alias_get_id(node, "usb") that fails probe when the node is not in
/aliases and then uses the id for nothing but an error string.

The ordering this changes is worth stating plainly, because hardware
will be the first thing to test it. The vendor's write lands in glue
probe, before the dwc3 child node is even created. Ours lands in
phy .init, which dwc3_core_init() calls at drivers/usb/dwc3/core.c
before dwc3_core_soft_reset() and well before dwc3_event_buffers_setup(),
so it precedes all DMA -- which is what bit 10 governs. It does not
precede everything: dwc3_core_probe() reads GSNPSID through
dwc3_core_is_valid() before dwc3_core_init() runs at all. That read
goes through the AXI slave port, which neither bit is documented to
affect, and this board runs dr_mode = "host", so bit 9 governs a data
path it never uses. If that reasoning is wrong the symptom is precise
and immediate -- "this is not a DesignWare USB3 DRD Core" -- and the
fix is to put the write back in a dwc3-of-simple-shaped glue.

The same write is also made once from probe. It is idempotent, so the
cost is a register access, and it closes a gap that .init alone leaves
open: the clock, the reset and VBUS all live in .power_on, and
phy_power_off() followed by phy_power_on() does not pass through .init.
dwc3 pairs init/exit around power_on/power_off, so nothing exercises
that today -- but a second consumer, or an .exit added to this driver
later, would re-assert and re-release the PHY reset with no .init left
to put the bit back.

Both 100 ms delays in power_on and the one after the endianness write
are the vendor's, unconditional and unexplained there. There is no
hardware reference manual for this SoC and no vendor support, so they
are reproduced rather than tuned; the AVM kernel is the only one known
to bring this block up.

Two things the vendor driver has are deliberately absent. Over-current
handling -- an IRQ, a workqueue and a pair of pinctrl states that cut
and restore port power -- is dead code on this hardware: the pads that
feed over-current detection are pin 7 for usb0 and pin 2 for usb1,
which on the FRITZ!Box 7560 are the DECT and WLAN buttons, which is
why the vendor board files delete all four of their own pinctrl_usb*
nodes and strip pinctrl-names/-0/-1 off both PHY nodes. And VBUS is a
proper optional regulator rather than the vendor's mandatory one:
devm_regulator_get() would answer an absent supply with the dummy
regulator, and a silently dummied VBUS is indistinguishable from
working hardware until nothing enumerates.

select RESET_LANTIQ is not redundant. It has no prompt outside
COMPILE_TEST and defaults to SOC_TYPE_XWAY, which INTEL_MIPS does not
select, so on this platform nothing reaches it unless a driver selects
it. A PHY driver that takes a reset control should not depend on an
unrelated driver being enabled to get a reset driver.

None of the C is the vendor's, but everything that makes it work is:
the two chip-top bit positions, the polarity override offsets and their
bit numbers, the three delays and the clock-then-reset-then-VBUS order
were all read out of the two AVM files named above and reproduced value
for value, and with no reference manual for this SoC they could not have
been arrived at any other way. The file header says so, and carries both
the Intel copyright that comes with those numbers and a line for the
port.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/drivers/phy/intel/Kconfig b/drivers/phy/intel/Kconfig
index 3dd146bc4ddd..e8ce2311b3ed 100644
--- a/drivers/phy/intel/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/phy/intel/Kconfig
@@ -19,6 +19,25 @@ config PHY_INTEL_GRX500_PCIE
 	  To compile this driver as a module, choose M here: the module
 	  will be called phy-intel-grx500-pcie.ko.
 
+config PHY_INTEL_GRX500_USB
+	tristate "Intel GRX500 USB PHY driver"
+	depends on INTEL_MIPS || COMPILE_TEST
+	depends on OF && HAS_IOMEM
+	select GENERIC_PHY
+	select MFD_SYSCON
+	select RESET_CONTROLLER
+	select RESET_LANTIQ
+	help
+	  Enable this to support the USB PHYs that feed the on-chip DWC3
+	  controllers of the Intel MIPS (xRX500/GRX500) SoC family. Besides
+	  its own gate clock, reset line and optional VBUS supply the driver
+	  owns the AXI endianness field its controller needs in the chip-top
+	  syscon, which on this big-endian SoC has to be set before the
+	  controller does any DMA.
+
+	  To compile this driver as a module, choose M here: the module
+	  will be called phy-intel-grx500-usb.ko.
+
 config PHY_INTEL_KEEMBAY_EMMC
 	tristate "Intel Keem Bay EMMC PHY driver"
 	depends on ARCH_KEEMBAY || COMPILE_TEST
diff --git a/drivers/phy/intel/Makefile b/drivers/phy/intel/Makefile
index 1093ec293610..e1682e9ae3bc 100644
--- a/drivers/phy/intel/Makefile
+++ b/drivers/phy/intel/Makefile
@@ -1,5 +1,6 @@
 # SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
 obj-$(CONFIG_PHY_INTEL_GRX500_PCIE)	+= phy-intel-grx500-pcie.o
+obj-$(CONFIG_PHY_INTEL_GRX500_USB)	+= phy-intel-grx500-usb.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_PHY_INTEL_KEEMBAY_EMMC)	+= phy-intel-keembay-emmc.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_PHY_INTEL_KEEMBAY_USB)	+= phy-intel-keembay-usb.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_PHY_INTEL_LGM_COMBO)	+= phy-intel-lgm-combo.o
diff --git a/drivers/phy/intel/phy-intel-grx500-usb.c b/drivers/phy/intel/phy-intel-grx500-usb.c
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..2a845f28b7d5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/phy/intel/phy-intel-grx500-usb.c
@@ -0,0 +1,284 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+/*
+ * Copyright (C) 2016-2017 Intel Corporation.
+ * Copyright (C) 2026 Grische <github@grische.xyz>
+ *
+ * USB PHY driver for the Intel GRX500 (xRX500) SoC family: the chip-top AXI
+ * endianness word, the lane polarity overrides and the clock, reset and VBUS
+ * ordering the controller needs.
+ *
+ * The register offsets, bit positions and settling delays are reproduced from
+ * the vendor BSP (drivers/phy/phy-grx500-usb.c and
+ * drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-grx500.c); there is no hardware reference manual for
+ * this SoC.
+ */
+
+#include <linux/bitops.h>
+#include <linux/clk.h>
+#include <linux/delay.h>
+#include <linux/io.h>
+#include <linux/mfd/syscon.h>
+#include <linux/mod_devicetable.h>
+#include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/phy/phy.h>
+#include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/property.h>
+#include <linux/regmap.h>
+#include <linux/regulator/consumer.h>
+#include <linux/reset.h>
+
+/*
+ * Per-controller AXI configuration word in the chip-top syscon. It selects
+ * the byte order the controller presents to the AXI fabric, which is why this
+ * PHY driver exists at all: nothing else owns that register.
+ */
+#define USB_CFG_DEV_ENDIAN		BIT(9)
+#define USB_CFG_HOST_ENDIAN		BIT(10)
+
+/*
+ * PHY PDI registers, relative to the PHY's own window. These two overrides
+ * are the only registers in it this driver ever touches; the vendor driver
+ * has no calibration, trim, impedance or squelch programming either.
+ */
+#define USB_PHY_TX_OVRD			0x4000
+#define  USB_PHY_TX_INVERT		BIT(3)
+#define USB_PHY_RX_OVRD			0x4014
+#define  USB_PHY_RX_INVERT		BIT(1)
+
+/*
+ * Three settling delays, all of them the vendor's, all of them
+ * unconditional and undocumented there: one after the endian write, one
+ * between enabling the gate clock and releasing the PHY from reset, and one
+ * between that and switching VBUS on. Nothing in the AVM 4.9 sources says
+ * what is being waited for, and there is no hardware reference manual for
+ * this SoC to check against, so they are reproduced as-is rather than
+ * guessed at. This is the only kernel that is known to bring the block up.
+ */
+#define USB_PHY_ENDIAN_SETTLE_MS	100
+#define USB_PHY_CLK_SETTLE_MS		100
+#define USB_PHY_RESET_SETTLE_MS		100
+
+struct intel_usbphy {
+	struct device *dev;
+	void __iomem *base;
+	struct regmap *chiptop;
+	unsigned int cfg_offset;
+	struct clk *clk;
+	struct reset_control *rst;
+	struct regulator *vbus;
+	struct phy *phy;
+	bool invert_tx;
+	bool invert_rx;
+};
+
+static void intel_usbphy_set_bit(struct intel_usbphy *priv, unsigned int reg,
+				 u32 bit)
+{
+	u32 val;
+
+	val = readl(priv->base + reg);
+	val |= bit;
+	writel(val, priv->base + reg);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Host port big endian, device port little. Idempotent, and called from
+ * two places on purpose -- see the comment at the probe-side call.
+ */
+static int intel_usbphy_set_endian(struct intel_usbphy *priv)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = regmap_update_bits(priv->chiptop, priv->cfg_offset,
+				 USB_CFG_HOST_ENDIAN | USB_CFG_DEV_ENDIAN,
+				 USB_CFG_HOST_ENDIAN);
+	if (ret)
+		dev_err(priv->dev, "failed to select the AXI byte order\n");
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static int intel_usbphy_init(struct phy *phy)
+{
+	struct intel_usbphy *priv = phy_get_drvdata(phy);
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = intel_usbphy_set_endian(priv);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	msleep(USB_PHY_ENDIAN_SETTLE_MS);
+
+	/*
+	 * Lane polarity overrides, for boards that route the differential
+	 * pair inverted. Absent properties leave the hardware default.
+	 */
+	if (priv->invert_tx)
+		intel_usbphy_set_bit(priv, USB_PHY_TX_OVRD, USB_PHY_TX_INVERT);
+
+	if (priv->invert_rx)
+		intel_usbphy_set_bit(priv, USB_PHY_RX_OVRD, USB_PHY_RX_INVERT);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int intel_usbphy_power_on(struct phy *phy)
+{
+	struct intel_usbphy *priv = phy_get_drvdata(phy);
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = clk_prepare_enable(priv->clk);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(priv->dev, "failed to enable the PHY gate clock\n");
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	msleep(USB_PHY_CLK_SETTLE_MS);
+
+	ret = reset_control_deassert(priv->rst);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(priv->dev, "failed to release the PHY reset\n");
+		goto err_disable_clk;
+	}
+
+	msleep(USB_PHY_RESET_SETTLE_MS);
+
+	if (priv->vbus) {
+		ret = regulator_enable(priv->vbus);
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(priv->dev, "failed to switch VBUS on\n");
+			goto err_assert_reset;
+		}
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+
+err_assert_reset:
+	reset_control_assert(priv->rst);
+err_disable_clk:
+	clk_disable_unprepare(priv->clk);
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static int intel_usbphy_power_off(struct phy *phy)
+{
+	struct intel_usbphy *priv = phy_get_drvdata(phy);
+
+	if (priv->vbus)
+		regulator_disable(priv->vbus);
+
+	reset_control_assert(priv->rst);
+	clk_disable_unprepare(priv->clk);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static const struct phy_ops intel_usbphy_ops = {
+	.init = intel_usbphy_init,
+	.power_on = intel_usbphy_power_on,
+	.power_off = intel_usbphy_power_off,
+	.owner = THIS_MODULE,
+};
+
+static int intel_usbphy_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
+{
+	struct device *dev = &pdev->dev;
+	struct device_node *np = dev->of_node;
+	struct phy_provider *phy_provider;
+	struct intel_usbphy *priv;
+	int ret;
+
+	priv = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(*priv), GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!priv)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	priv->dev = dev;
+
+	priv->base = devm_platform_ioremap_resource(pdev, 0);
+	if (IS_ERR(priv->base))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(priv->base),
+				     "failed to map the PHY registers\n");
+
+	priv->chiptop = syscon_regmap_lookup_by_phandle(np, "intel,syscon");
+	if (IS_ERR(priv->chiptop))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(priv->chiptop),
+				     "failed to get the CHIPTOP syscon\n");
+
+	ret = device_property_read_u32(dev, "intel,syscon-offset",
+				       &priv->cfg_offset);
+	if (ret)
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "missing intel,syscon-offset\n");
+
+	priv->clk = devm_clk_get(dev, NULL);
+	if (IS_ERR(priv->clk))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(priv->clk),
+				     "failed to get the PHY gate clock\n");
+
+	priv->rst = devm_reset_control_get_exclusive(dev, "phy");
+	if (IS_ERR(priv->rst))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(priv->rst),
+				     "failed to get the PHY reset\n");
+
+	/*
+	 * VBUS is optional: port power is not software-controlled on every
+	 * board.
+	 */
+	priv->vbus = devm_regulator_get_optional(dev, "vbus");
+	if (IS_ERR(priv->vbus)) {
+		if (PTR_ERR(priv->vbus) != -ENODEV)
+			return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(priv->vbus),
+					     "failed to get the VBUS supply\n");
+
+		dev_dbg(dev, "no VBUS supply, assuming port power is fixed\n");
+		priv->vbus = NULL;
+	}
+
+	priv->invert_tx = device_property_present(dev, "intel,invert-tx-polarity");
+	priv->invert_rx = device_property_present(dev, "intel,invert-rx-polarity");
+
+
+	priv->phy = devm_phy_create(dev, np, &intel_usbphy_ops);
+	if (IS_ERR(priv->phy))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(priv->phy),
+				     "failed to create the PHY\n");
+
+	phy_set_drvdata(priv->phy, priv);
+
+	/*
+	 * The byte order must be set before the controller issues its first
+	 * AXI transfer, which is earlier than .init runs, so it is programmed
+	 * here too.
+	 */
+	ret = intel_usbphy_set_endian(priv);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	phy_provider = devm_of_phy_provider_register(dev, of_phy_simple_xlate);
+	if (IS_ERR(phy_provider))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(phy_provider),
+				     "failed to register the PHY provider\n");
+
+	dev_dbg(dev, "USB PHY probed, AXI config at CHIPTOP + 0x%x\n",
+		priv->cfg_offset);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static const struct of_device_id intel_usbphy_match[] = {
+	{ .compatible = "intel,grx500-usb-phy" },
+	{}
+};
+MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, intel_usbphy_match);
+
+static struct platform_driver intel_usbphy_driver = {
+	.probe = intel_usbphy_probe,
+	.driver = {
+		.name = "intel-grx500-usb-phy",
+		.of_match_table = intel_usbphy_match,
+	},
+};
+module_platform_driver(intel_usbphy_driver);
+
+MODULE_DESCRIPTION("Intel GRX500 USB PHY driver");
+MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
